22:53:09 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a really fair question—and it gets to the heart of the difference. Yes, electricity is the same stuff whether it’s powering a car or a heater. But the reason the “cheaper” logic doesn’t always carry over is because *you’re comparing against different opponents* and the conversion steps are different. ### The car: electricity vs. gasoline - A gasoline engine wastes about 70–80% of the fuel’s energy as heat; only ~20–30% actually moves the car. - An electric motor uses ~85–90% of the electricity to move the car. - So even if electricity costs a bit more per unit of raw energy, the electric car needs so much **less total energy** to go a mile that it ends up cheaper per mile. **Example:** Gasoline contains ~33.7 kWh per gallon. If your car gets 30 mpg, you need about 1.12 kWh of *gasoline energy* per mile. But because the engine is inefficient, you burn more like 1.12 kWh per mile. An electric car might need only 0.25–0.35 kWh of *electricity* to go that same mile (after efficiency). At $3.50/gallon vs. $0.15/kWh electricity, that’s $0.12/mile for gas vs. $0.04–0.05/mile for electric. A clear win. ### The house: electricity vs. natural gas (or oil) Now let’s heat your house. The competition is no longer gasoline—it’s usually natural gas, and the physics + pricing are very different: - **A modern gas furnace is already ~95% efficient** at turning fuel into heat inside your home. You burn 1 unit of gas energy, you get ~0.95 units of heat. - **Electric resistance heating** (baseboards, space heaters) is 100% efficient at the appliance, but that electricity had to be generated somewhere. If it came from a natural gas power plant that’s only 35–40% efficient, then *from the original fuel to your home* you might have thrown away 60% or more of the energy. So each kWh of heat from a resistance heater required burning a lot more original fuel than a high-efficiency furnace. - **Heat pumps** are different: they can move 2–4 times as much heat as the electricity they consume, giving them an effective efficiency of 200–400%. In many climates they beat gas on operating cost, *if electricity is reasonably priced*. But in very cold weather, most air-source heat pumps lose efficiency and may need expensive backup resistance heat. And the upfront cost is higher. Crucially, **natural gas is often dirt cheap per unit of energy delivered**. In the U.S., residential natural gas can cost around $1.00–$1.50 per therm (29.3 kWh equivalent). That’s $0.034–0.051 per kWh of heat energy. Residential electricity averages around $0.13–0.20 per kWh. Even with a furnace only 95% efficient, gas heat often costs **half or a third** of what simple electric resistance heat would cost. A heat pump with a COP of 3 might bring the cost down to parity or better in mild climates, but not always—and it depends heavily on local electricity and gas prices. ### Why the difference? - The car’s opponent (gasoline) is **wildly inefficient** in converting fuel to motion, making electricity’s efficiency advantage enormous. - The home heating opponent (natural gas) is **already extremely efficient** at converting fuel to heat, and the electricity delivery chain involves its own conversion losses unless you’re using a heat pump. Plus, gas is often artificially cheap due to abundant supply and legacy infrastructure. ### The bottom line Electricity *is* the same stuff, but the economic case depends on what you’re replacing and how efficiently you can use each fuel for that specific job. For cars, you’re swapping a wasteful process for a lean one. For home heating, you’re often pitting a directly burned, cheap fuel against electricity that may carry generation losses—unless you’ve got a heat pump and the right climate/electricity rates. That’s why even as electric cars surge, many homes still find gas heat cheaper, at least for now.
